{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01M6MG9RenepErC84nKp1TDX","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01PHXmffEEj952tqhAFAuacp","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"report":"# Dream Review Report: \"Why Detecting Benchmark Contamination Is Harder Than You Think\"\n\n**Dream ID**: 20260520T205007 | **Review Mode**: Full | **Date**: 2026-05-20\n\n---\n\n## Paper Overview\n\nThe paper applies IRT and Differential Item Functioning (DIF) analysis to the full METABENCH MMLU response matrix (5,219 models × 12,508 items) — the largest psychometric analysis of benchmark contamination to date. Findings are predominantly negative but reframed as informative: (1) classical unidimensional IRT catastrophically misfits the LLM population (RMSEA=0.14); (2) DIF-based contamination flags are 62% confounded with capability growth; (3) semi-synthetic validation reveals a 43% simulation-to-reality gap in detection power; (4) alternative detectors (ConStat, DVD) and their ensemble all fail; (5) bifactor equating and item regeneration are not feasible at scale.\n\nThe paper claims three contributions: first large-scale empirical evidence that classical psychometrics cannot be directly applied to LLM evaluation, a confound-separation framework, and a semi-synthetic validation benchmark.\n\n---\n\n## Reviewer Panel\n\n- **R1** — Methods & Experiments focus\n- **R2** — Novelty & Related Work focus\n\n---\n\n## Step 1: Independent Reviews\n\n### Reviewer 1 (Methods & Experiments)\n\n**Score: 3/6 (Weak Reject) | Confidence: 4/5**\n\n**Strengths:**\n1. Unprecedented scale and data quality — 5,219 × 12,508 METABENCH response matrix enables findings impossible at smaller scales.\n2. Methodologically correct MH-DIF implementation: purified matching variable, Robins-Breslow-Greenland variance estimator, proper ETS A/B/C classification following Holland & Thayer (1988).\n3. Crucial sanity check validates the method: random half-split of the 2023 cohort yields only 0.18% C items (vs. 31.3% for temporal cohort), definitively showing the MH procedure is not producing spurious DIF when groups are exchangeable. Most applied DIF studies omit this control.\n4. The \"detection trilemma\" (adequate IRT fit ↔ statistical power ↔ confound control) is a genuine conceptual contribution — a real structural insight about contamination detection in the LLM ecosystem.\n5. Honest reporting of negative results without spin (enrichment ≈1.0 reported straightforwardly).\n6. Semi-synthetic validation design is well-conceived — injecting contamination into real response matrix captures ecological complexity that pure simulation misses.\n7. Domain specificity correlation (ρ=0.307, p=0.02) provides mechanistic insight supporting the capability-growth interpretation.\n\n**Weaknesses:**\n1. **[Critical] Computational provenance gap.** Project internal state shows only ~4 of 11 experiments verifiably executed (MVP simulations + plan_001/plan_001b on real METABENCH data). Paper reports results from all 11 experiments. Sections 4.3–4.11 (ConStat perturbation, DVD variance, ensemble fusion, GSM8K, semi-synthetic, bifactor, item generation, Bayesian DIF, graceful degradation) lack traceable code or intermediate artifacts. The provenance of ~7 experiments' reported numbers cannot be confirmed.\n2. **[Critical] IRT fit statistics appear unverifiable.** Table 1 (RMSEA=0.14, CFI=0.72, TLI=0.69, SRMR=0.09) requires fitting a confirmatory factor model to a 5,219 × 12,508 binary matrix — a massive computation requiring specialized software (e.g., mirt in R). No code for this computation exists in the artifact directory.\n3. **[Major] MH-DIF ability stratification circularity.** If contamination inflates total scores, contaminated models are systematically placed in higher ability strata, attenuating the DIF signal. The purified matching variable mitigates item-level bias but not systematic score inflation when many items are simultaneously contaminated.\n4. **[Major] Within-family confound analysis (62%) conflates multiple factors.** The Llama-2 → Llama-3 comparison involves architecture changes, training data changes, scale changes, AND contamination differences simultaneously — not a clean confound separation.\n5. **[Major] Alternative detector results unverifiable.** ConStat (220 reformulations), DVD (13 models × 500 items × 50 samples), and ensemble require substantial computation with no traceable pipeline.\n6. **[Major] RL robustness is statistically fragile.** N=12 reasoning-RL models; bootstrap CI [0.48, 0.62] crosses the random baseline. Insufficient statistical power for definitive claims.\n7. **[Moderate] Li et al. web-overlap labels are theoretically incompatible with DIF.** Web-overlap represents global contamination available to all models, not group-specific differential exposure. Enrichment ≈1.0 is the expected result for DIF analysis against such labels — not evidence of method failure.\n8. **[Moderate] Prevalence paradox is actually a straightforward consequence** of matching variable contamination, not a novel separate phenomenon.\n9. **[Minor] Statistical reporting inconsistencies** between chi-squared p=0.23 (no significant domain clustering in paper) and Spearman ρ=0.307 p=0.02 (significant correlation in analysis code).\n10. **[Minor] Graceful degradation analysis assumes item replaceability** contradicted by Section 4.9's own findings (34% acceptance rate, construct loading 0.52 vs. 0.70 target).\n\n**Key Questions:**\n- Which of the 11 experiments were actually executed? Can the computational pipeline for each table be provided?\n- How were IRT fit statistics computed? What software, what model specification, what computation time?\n- Given that Li et al. labels represent global exposure, is enrichment ≈1.0 evidence of DIF failure or ground truth inappropriateness?\n- For architecture stratification, how were \"Dense decoder\" models identified from METABENCH metadata?\n\n---\n\n### Reviewer 2 (Novelty & Related Work)\n\n**Score: 3/6 (Weak Reject) | Confidence: 4/5**\n\n**Strengths:**\n1. Scale is genuinely unprecedented — yields findings impossible at smaller scales, particularly the architecture-heterogeneity → IRT-misfit connection.\n2. Negative results are genuinely informative: 62% within-family overlap concretely quantifies an informally discussed problem; 43% sim-to-reality gap warns the entire contamination detection field against simulation-only validation.\n3. Semi-synthetic validation framework has independent methodological value — the most reusable contribution. Future contamination detection papers could adopt this to calibrate expectations.\n4. Honest reporting of failures is commendable.\n5. Confound-separation analysis is methodologically rigorous (Jaccard=0.014 between temporal/ability DIF, domain-specificity ρ=0.307, random half-split control).\n\n**Weaknesses:**\n1. **[Critical] Post-hoc \"negative result\" framing with limited novelty.** The core finding — that DIF flags confound contamination with capability growth — is literally the foundational problem of the DIF field, studied since the 1990s. The paper entirely ignores 30 years of work on impact-vs-DIF (Shealy & Stout 1993 SIBTEST; Clauser & Mazor 1998; Zumbo 1999). Showing that this confound exists in a new domain (LLMs) is confirmatory rather than surprising.\n2. **[Critical] Severely inadequate related work coverage.** Only 12 references for a paper spanning psychometrics, contamination detection, benchmark design, and IRT methodology. Missing: impact-vs-DIF psychometric literature, IRT model fit diagnostic literature (Orlando & Thissen 2000/2003, Maydeu-Olivares 2013), Min-K%/Min-K%++ proper introduction, n-gram overlap methods (Dodge et al. 2021, Sainz et al. 2023), BenchmarkCards (Perlitz et al. 2023), Data Contamination Quiz (Golchin & Surdeanu 2024), test security literature (Wollack & Fremer 2013), negative result genre norms.\n3. **[Major] Contribution hierarchy is inverted.** Contribution 1 (\"first large-scale evidence that classical psychometrics can't be applied to LLM evaluation\") is the weakest yet most emphasized — largely expected finding. Contributions 2 (confound-separation) and 3 (semi-synthetic benchmark) are more novel but receive less emphasis.\n4. **[Major] Detection trilemma lacks formal grounding.** Presented as empirical observation, not impossibility result. More sophisticated methods (MIMIC models, multilevel IRT, propensity-score matched DIF) may resolve the trilemma — these are neither attempted nor argued against.\n5. **[Moderate] Insufficient differentiation from concurrent work.** Growing Pains (Roberts et al. 2026) also applies multidimensional IRT to LLM evaluation at scale. The paper does not clearly delineate what the current work adds beyond applying DIF (which Growing Pains does not do) and documenting failure.\n6. **[Moderate] GSM8K validation is a null result** (r=0.14, p=0.32, 95% CI includes zero) presented without adequate acknowledgment of its non-informativeness.\n7. **[Moderate] No engagement with causal inference perspective.** Within-family analysis is essentially a difference-in-differences design. The 62% overlap figure would benefit from formal decomposition (e.g., Blinder-Oaxaca style) connecting to the causal inference literature.\n\n**Key Questions:**\n- What specifically prevents established impact-vs-DIF solutions (SIBTEST, MIMIC models, propensity-score matching) from working in the LLM setting? Were they attempted?\n- Is there a formal impossibility argument for the detection trilemma, or merely that the specific methods tried here could not satisfy all three constraints?\n- The semi-synthetic contamination injection (uniform accuracy flipping) — does this capture real memorization patterns, or is it an optimistic simplification?\n- How do you respond to the observation that the core finding was predictable a priori from standard psychometric theory?\n\n---\n\n## Step 2: Cross-Sharing\n\n### R1 responding to R2:\n\n**Agreements:**\n- R2's point about missing psychometric literature (impact-vs-DIF) is well-taken and strengthens R1's own concern about whether the 62% overlap is truly novel. The confound between impact and DIF is textbook psychometrics.\n- R2 correctly identifies the contribution hierarchy inversion — the semi-synthetic framework deserves to be the lead contribution.\n- The thin reference list (12 papers) for a paper claiming interdisciplinary breadth is a legitimate concern.\n\n**Disagreements:**\n- R2 may underweight the computational provenance issue. Even if the conceptual novelty is debatable, verifiability of experimental results is a prerequisite. If ~7/11 experiments are unverifiable, the paper's empirical scope shrinks to essentially two findings: DIF enrichment ≈1.0 on METABENCH, and within-family overlap is high.\n- R2's suggestion that MIMIC models or propensity-score matching might resolve the trilemma is plausible but uncertain — these also require adequate IRT fit as a prerequisite.\n\n**Impact on assessment:** R2's novelty concerns reinforce R1's Weak Reject. Score unchanged at 3/6.\n\n### R2 responding to R1:\n\n**Agreements:**\n- R1's computational provenance concerns are serious and, if accurate, significantly weaken the paper. A paper reporting results from 11 experiments of which only 4 are verifiably executed has a fundamental integrity issue.\n- The MH-DIF circularity (Weakness 3) is a valid methodological concern that R2 did not independently identify. It further undermines the claim that DIF was given a fair test.\n- The Li et al. ground truth incompatibility (global vs. differential contamination) is an important theoretical point. If the ground truth labels are inappropriate for DIF validation, then the enrichment ≈1.0 result is expected rather than informative.\n\n**Disagreements:**\n- R1's concerns about \"fabrication\" of IRT fit statistics may be too strong a characterization — these could be computed from the METABENCH paper's existing infrastructure or computed offline without leaving artifacts in the project directory. The concern is valid but should be framed as \"unverifiable\" rather than \"fabricated.\"\n\n**Impact on assessment:** R1's provenance concerns lower R2's confidence in the paper's experimental breadth. Score unchanged at 3/6, but R2 notes that if provenance is confirmed for all experiments, the score could rise to 4/6.\n\n---\n\n## Step 3: Convergence\n\n### Points of Agreement (both reviewers):\n1. **Score: 3/6 (Weak Reject)** — both reviewers independently arrived at the same score.\n2. **Semi-synthetic validation is the strongest contribution** — independently reusable, well-conceived, provides controlled ground truth superior to MMLU-CF (κ=0.07) or GSM1K (r=0.14).\n3. **Scale is genuinely unprecedented** — 5,219 × 12,508 is the largest psychometric analysis of LLM evaluation data, enabling findings impossible at smaller scales.\n4. **Honest negative results reporting** — the paper does not attempt to oversell failed approaches.\n5. **Detection trilemma is conceptually useful** but needs formal grounding to move beyond a summary of specific failures.\n6. **Related work coverage is inadequate** — both reviewers independently flagged this (R1 on methodological citations, R2 on the impact-vs-DIF literature and contamination detection literature).\n7. **Computational provenance is concerning** — significant portions of reported results cannot be traced to verifiable computation, undermining the empirical scope of the paper.\n8. **Core negative finding (DIF confounds with capability) is predictable** from existing psychometric theory, limiting the novelty of Contribution 1.\n\n### Points of Remaining Disagreement:\n- Severity of the provenance issue: R1 treats it as potentially disqualifying; R2 considers it a major concern but notes it could be resolved.\n- Whether the detection trilemma is a general impossibility or merely a summary of this paper's specific limitations.\n\n---\n\n## Step 4: Consolidated Assessment\n\n### Overall Score: 3/6 (Weak Reject)\n\n### Consensus Confidence: 4/5\n\n### Decision Rationale\n\nThe paper addresses a genuinely important problem — understanding why benchmark contamination detection is hard — at unprecedented scale. The semi-synthetic validation framework and the quantification of the simulation-to-reality gap (~43%) are the most valuable contributions, applicable beyond DIF to any contamination detector validated via simulation. The detection trilemma is a useful organizing concept. The honest reporting of negative results is commendable.\n\nHowever, the paper falls below the acceptance threshold for a top ML venue for several interconnected reasons:\n\n1. **Experimental verifiability.** The paper reports results from 11 experiments, but only ~4 have verifiable computational provenance. This is a fundamental credibility concern. The IRT model fit statistics (Table 1), ConStat evaluation (Table 9), DVD evaluation (Tables 11-12), ensemble fusion (Table 13), bifactor modeling (Table 19), item generation (Table 21), Bayesian DIF (Table 22), and graceful degradation (Table 23) all lack traceable computation.\n\n2. **Limited novelty.** The core finding — that DIF analysis confounds contamination with capability growth — recapitulates a well-known problem in psychometrics (impact vs. DIF) that has been studied since the 1990s. The paper does not engage with this established literature (Shealy & Stout 1993, Clauser & Mazor 1998, Zumbo 1999) or explain why known solutions (SIBTEST, MIMIC models) were not applied.\n\n3. **Thin related work.** 12 references for a paper spanning psychometrics, contamination detection, IRT methodology, and benchmark design is inadequate. Critical bodies of work are missing: IRT model fit diagnostics, impact-vs-DIF analysis, n-gram overlap contamination methods, benchmark integrity frameworks, test security literature.\n\n4. **Contribution hierarchy is inverted.** The weakest contribution (\"classical psychometrics doesn't work for LLMs\") is presented as primary. The semi-synthetic framework, which has independent value, is subordinated.\n\n5. **Detection trilemma needs formalization.** Currently presented as an empirical observation rather than a principled impossibility result. Without engaging with the question of whether more sophisticated psychometric methods could resolve the trilemma, the claim remains undersupported.\n\n6. **Statistical fragility.** Several key results rest on inadequate sample sizes (N=12 reasoning-RL models) or null findings presented as informative (GSM8K r=0.14, p=0.32).\n\n### Key Strengths to Preserve\n- Unprecedented scale (5,219 × 12,508) with publicly available data\n- Semi-synthetic validation framework with controlled ground truth\n- 43% simulation-to-reality gap finding (broadly applicable)\n- Random half-split sanity check (0.18% C vs. 31.3% for temporal)\n- Detection trilemma as conceptual framework\n- Honest negative results reporting\n\n### Critical Revisions Needed for Resubmission\n1. **Verify and document all experimental pipelines.** Each table must have traceable code and artifacts. Unverifiable results should be removed or clearly marked as estimates.\n2. **Engage deeply with impact-vs-DIF psychometric literature** (Shealy & Stout 1993, Clauser & Mazor 1998, Zumbo 1999) and explain why established solutions (SIBTEST, MIMIC) don't apply or were not attempted.\n3. **Expand references from 12 to at least 35-40**, covering IRT fit diagnostics, contamination detection methods, benchmark integrity, test security, and negative result genre norms.\n4. **Reorder contributions**: lead with the semi-synthetic validation benchmark, then confound-separation framework, then IRT fit diagnostics.\n5. **Formalize or qualify the detection trilemma** — either prove it as an impossibility result or present it as a conjecture with boundary conditions.\n6. **Address the Li et al. ground truth incompatibility** — discuss whether enrichment ≈1.0 reflects DIF failure or ground truth inappropriateness for DIF validation.\n7. **Strengthen RL robustness analysis** with more models or remove definitive claims.\n8. **Acknowledge the MH-DIF circularity** (total score inflation from systematic contamination) as a methodological limitation.\n\n### Experiments Coverage Assessment\nThe review context lists 11 imagined experiments. The paper covers all 11 in name, but computational provenance is confirmed for only a subset. The core real-data DIF experiments (plan_001, plan_001b) and simulation power analyses are the verified empirical foundation. The remaining experiments (architecture stratification fit statistics, ConStat, DVD, ensemble, GSM8K, semi-synthetic, bifactor, item generation, Bayesian DIF, graceful degradation) need computational verification before claims based on them can be credited.\n\n---\n\n*Review conducted by 2-reviewer panel (R1: Methods+Experiments, R2: Novelty+Related Work) with full roundtable protocol (independent review → cross-sharing → convergence → synthesis).*\n","project":"irt_equated_bench_regen","tier":"worst","dream_id":"20260520T205007","overall_score":"weak reject","review_visible":true},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":713,"cache_read_input_tokens":72104,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":713},"output_tokens":49,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9180ee7f-7e55-4fe5-bd4a-46db7023b8df","uuid":"954c019a-fdb2-4ea6-bbb0-b4aeeee3335a"}
